area under two queues of one request a cycle each (unit test, depth 8): the queue fills to 7 after a cycle (room is judged by the count before the pop, 13a); from then on every freed slot goes to the write (writes win the tie, ruled 2 October), so the SRAM read queue waits while the writes continue [meas: MemTest queue depth and stalls]
write combining (param, off by default): after a congested stretch, 6 of 26 64-bit writes merged into a queued write to the same row [meas: MemTest write combining]
read latency, request put into the SRAM's holding register at t -> row on the return line [meas: MemTest read latency]: L=1: 3 L=2: 4 L=3: 5 L=4: 6 = 2 + L: taken at t + 1, served at t + 2, row visible at t + 2 + L; the SRAM read queue releases the packet in that cycle
throughput [meas: MemTest throughput], 2,000 requests, cycles from the first packet to the last at the receiver:
  64-bit reads, one area                                                  2000 cycles for 2000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
  64-bit reads, round-robin over 8 areas                                  2000 cycles for 2000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
  128-bit reads, one area (2 packets each)                                4000 cycles for 4000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
  32-bit reads, one area                                                  2000 cycles for 2000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
  64-bit reads (area 1) + 2,000 incoming writes (area 2), together        2000 cycles for 2000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
  reads every 2nd cycle (area 1) + writes every 2nd cycle (area 1, another buffer)  3999 cycles for 2000 packets; read stalls 0, write stalls 0
priority: with requests from all three clients waiting, the SRAM read queue issues gather value, address generator, read port in that order; 50 back-to-back gather values hold the address generator's request back 50 cycles [meas: MemTest priority]
return order: 200 reads alternating a busy area (also taking bursts of 8 writes) and a free one leave in request order; read latency max 8 (unloaded 4), write stalls 0 [meas: MemTest return order]
token window: 128-bit reads issued at one a cycle return at one per 2 cycles; in flight peaks at 64 of 64, then issue waits (177 cycles) [meas: MemTest token window]
disjoint-buffer check: a phase that reads one buffer while another tile's writes land in it is reported once per tile and phase, e.g. "tile 50: chunk 0x40000 written after being read in the same phase (invariant 2, phase 2)" [meas: MemTest disjoint-buffer check]
